  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "3.0.0: Breaking: this version uses file format 5, and files written by 2.5.1 and earlier (format 4) do not open. This package has no converter, so convert existing files before upgrading. The assembly version is now 3.0.0.0, so applications built against 2.x must be recompiled or given a binding redirect. New: RizeDb now also targets .NET Standard 2.0, tested on .NET Framework 4.8, and files read the same on both targets. That target depends on System.Memory, System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe, System.Threading.Tasks.Extensions, System.Reflection.Emit and System.Reflection.Emit.Lightweight; .NET 10 has no dependencies. Crash safety: format 5 adds a checksummed write-ahead log and checkpoints, so a store recovers its committed data after a power cut. New ObjectStoreOptions controls background log syncing, SyncLog forces a sync, and MappedFlushMode.Full makes commits on a mapped file durable. Failed writes: a failed write no longer stops the store for good. The write throws and is not stored, and the next call restores the store from its log. A store recovers once; a second failure, a failed recovery or a failed disk sync makes it refuse further work until the file is reopened. New WriteCommittedException means a write was stored and a later step failed, so don't retry it. New ObjectStore.Recovered event reports each recovery. Fixes: a writer queued behind a failing commit could be told it succeeded while its record was lost (Release builds). A read that runs out of memory or system resources, a throwing query predicate, mixed-type range bounds, and an Update with an unknown property or wrong-typed value no longer put the store into its bad state, and the refusal now names the original cause. Query fixes include Nullable HasValue, negated Any, Any and Contains over a null sequence, and document queries on unindexable types. Also new: Verify checks a store's integrity, and document collections work in transaction groups.",
